.Dd 2026-10-24
.Dt b64 3
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm b64
.Nd a library for encoding ASCII to Base64 and decoding Base64 to ASCII
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Lb b64
.In b64.h
.Ft char *
.Fn asctob64 "const char *ascii" "bool urlencode"
.Ft char *
.Fn b64toasc "const char *b64"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The b64 library is a simple library with functions to convert an ASCII string
to a Base64 encoded string and a Base64 encoded string to an ASCII string.
.Pp
The function
.Fn asctob64
takes two arguments. The first,
.Fa ascii ,
is a pointer to an ASCII string that is to be encoded. The second argument,
.Fa urlencode ,
is a boolean value that if set to
.Em true ,
instructs the function to use URL-safe encoding rules for the string. when
URL-safe encoding is performed, the Base64 characters minus and underline are
used instead of the plus and solidus (slash) characters. The function returns
according pointer to the Base64 encoded string. The memory for the string is
allocated in the function, and the caller must use
.Xr free 3
when they no longer need the string.
.Pp
The function
.Fn b64toasc
takes only a single argument,
.Fa b64 ,
which is a pointer to a Base64 encoded string. The function returns a pointer
to a decoded ASCII string. The memory for the string in allocated in the
function, and should be freed by the caller when no longer required. It does
not matter whether the Base64 encoded string is encoded using URL-safe
Base64 characters.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
The two library functions return NULL-terminated character strings. Each string
is allocated in the function, and it is the responsibility of the caller to
free the string when it is no longer required.
.Sh EXAMPLES
The following examples show how to convert an ASCII string to a Base64-encoded
string, and an encoded Base-64 string to an ASCII string.
.Ss Encoding ASCII to Base64
.Bd -literal -offset
char *asciistr = "Hello, world!", *base64str;

base64str = asctob64 (asciistr, false);
.Ed
.Pp
.Ss Decoding Base64 to ASCII
.Bd -literal -offset
char *asciistr, *base64str = "SGVsbG8sIHdvcmxkIQ==";

asciistr = b64toasc (base64str);
.Ed
.Sh ERRORS
.Pp
The two library functions return a string. If the returned pointer is NULL, the
library was unable to allocate memory using
.Xr malloc 3 .
.Sh STANDARDS
.Pp
The library implements Base64 encoding and decoding according to the Internet
Engineering Task Force (IETF) Request For Comments (RFC) 4648 "The Base16,
Base32, and Base64 Data Encodings" standard.
.Pp
